---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import DeviceFrame from "../components/DeviceFrame.astro";
import { playUrl } from "../site";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<Layout lang="en" path="" description="Hikkonuki is a free Android app for importing camera photos and videos over USB. Copy from a DJI Osmo Pocket 4P or Insta360 Ace Pro 2, shrink compatible 4K videos to 2K, and share from your gallery.">
  <div class="cols">
    <div>
      <h1>Import camera videos to Android over USB</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Hikkonuki is a free Android app that imports photos and videos from a gimbal or action camera over USB.
        It can shrink compatible 4K videos to 2K as it saves them to your phone, ready to share from your gallery or back up with Google Photos.
      </p>
      <p class="lead">
        That is the whole app. No ads, no account, no feed, no editor.
        It doesn't even have permission to use the internet.
      </p>
      <p class="home-actions">
        <a class="play-badge" href={playUrl}>
          <img src={`${base}play-badge/en.png`} width="646" height="250" alt="Get it on Google Play" />
        </a>
        <a href={`${base}usb-import/`}>How it works</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Camera connection guides</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><a href={`${base}osmo-pocket/`}>DJI Osmo Pocket 4P USB transfer to Android</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${base}insta360/`}>Insta360 Ace Pro 2 USB transfer to Android</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><a href={`${base}usb-import/`}>USB import</a>: plug the camera in and its photos and videos are listed. It reads the camera as USB storage, over the cable.</li>
        <li><a href={`${base}browse/`}>Browse</a>: grid or list, long-press to select several, swipe through the details.</li>
        <li><a href={`${base}video-slim/`}>2K conversion</a>: compatible 4K video is scaled to a long edge of 2560. The app prefers HEVC and keeps 10-bit video where the phone can encode it.</li>
        <li><a href={`${base}export/`}>Export</a>: keeps going after you leave the app, and copes with the cable being pulled halfway.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Three steps</h2>
      <table class="spec">
        <tr><th>1. Plug in</th><td>Connect the camera to the phone with a USB-C cable. The app opens on the camera's photos and videos.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>2. Pick</th><td>Tap one to look at it, or long-press to select as many as you want.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3. Export</th><td>Photos are copied as they are. Compatible 4K videos are converted to 2K. Both land in <code>DCIM/Hikkonuki</code>, where the gallery and Google Photos see them.</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="note">The name is from the Japanese <i lang="ja">hikkonuku</i>, to yank something out.</p>
    </div>
    <DeviceFrame src={`${base}shots/en/08_selection.webp`} alt="Three items selected in the grid, with an Export button" caption="Select what you want and export. Videos show their length." />
  </div>
</Layout>
